L001163	
L001164	\title{ \quad\\[0.5cm] Searches for charged-lepton-flavor violation in $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ decays}
L001165	
L001166	\input{pub097-orcid.tex}
L001167	
L001168	\begin{abstract}
L001169	  We report the first searches for charged-lepton-flavor violation in decays of $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ ($J=0, 1,$ and $2$) to a pair of charged leptons
L001170	  using 158 million $\Upsilon(2S)$ decays collected with the Belle detector in $e^+e^-$ collisions at the KEKB collider.
L001171	  No significant signal is observed, and we set upper limits on the branching fractions for $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ decays
L001172	  to $e^\pm\mu^\mp$ at the level of $10^{-6}$ and to $e^\pm\tau^\mp$ or $\mu^\pm\tau^\mp$ at the level of $10^{-5}$.
L001173	  Limits on $\chi_{b0}(1P)$ decays are translated into bounds on the corresponding Wilson coefficients of scalar operators that mediate charged-lepton-flavor violation.
L001174	\keywords{Quarkonia decays, charged-lepton-flavor violation}
L001175	\end{abstract}
L001176	
L001177	\pacs{11.30.Hv, 12.60.-i, 13.20.Gd, 13.25.Gv, 14.60.-z}
L001178	
L001179	\maketitle
L001180	
L001181	
L001182	Charged-lepton-flavor violation (CLFV) is an unambiguous signature of physics beyond the standard model (SM)~\cite{Raidal:2008jk,deGouvea:2013zba,Celis:2014asa}.
L001183	Extensive searches for CLFV in decays of the $\tau$ lepton~\cite{BaBar:2009hkt,Belle:2021ysv},
L001184	mesons~\cite{Alonso:2015sja,Abada:2015zea}, and the Higgs boson~\cite{CMS:2021rsq,ATLAS:2023mvd} have placed stringent limits on new interactions.
L001185	CLFV has so far been searched for in decays of pseudoscalar mesons like $\pi^0$~\cite{NA62:2021zxl} and $\eta$~\cite{White:1995jc} and vector quarkonia such as $J/\psi$~\cite{BESIII:2021slj} and $\Upsilon(nS)$~\cite{BaBar:2010vxb,BaBar:2021loj,Belle:2022cce,Dhamija:2024}.
L001186	
L001187	In this Letter, we report the first searches for CLFV in $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ decays with $J=0, 1,$ and $2$.
L001188	The $\chi_{b0}(1P)$ decays provide a unique low-energy probe of CLFV in the scalar sector, complementary to direct searches for CLFV in Higgs boson decays.
L001189	Similarly, the $\chi_{b1}(1P)$ and $\chi_{b2}(1P)$ decays provide the first searches for CLFV in decays of axial-vector and tensor particles, respectively~\cite{Hazard:2016fnc}.
L001190	Although not expected within the SM, interactions beyond the SM can induce
L001191	$\cll{J}$ with $\ell_1\neq\ell_2$ and $\ell_1,\ell_2=e,\mu,\tau$~\footnote{Charge-conjugate decay modes are implied throughout.}
L001192	at experimentally observable rates~\cite{Hazard:2016fnc}, providing strong motivation for these searches.
L001193	The observation of any such decay would clearly indicate the presence of new particles and interactions beyond the SM.
L001194	
L001195	The $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ states are copiously produced via $\Upsilon(2S)\to \gamma\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ in $e^+e^-$ collisions.
L001196	This study uses a sample of $(158 \pm 4) \times 10^6$ $\y2$ mesons~\cite{Wang:2011ny2}, the largest collected to date, produced in 24.7~$\fb$ of data recorded by the Belle detector~\cite{Belle:2000cnh, Belle:2012iwr}
L001197	at a center-of-mass energy of 10.023\gev at the KEKB collider~\cite{Kurokawa:2001nw,Abe:2013fma}.
L001198	
L001199	Several decays are simulated to estimate reconstruction efficiencies and background contributions.
L001200	We simulate $\y2$ decays using EVTGEN~\cite{Lange:2001uf},
L001201	leptonic $\cb{J}$ decays using the multi-body phase-space decay model,
L001202	radiative decays using the helicity-amplitude decay model,
L001203	and leptonic $\y1$ decays using the vector-meson-to-dilepton decay model.
L001204	Processes such as $e^+e^- \to \ell^+\ell^-$, $q\bar{q}~(q=u,d,s,c)$, $\gamma\gamma$, and $\gamma q\bar{q}$
L001205	that potentially contribute to backgrounds are simulated using PYTHIA~6.4~\cite{Sjöstrand_2006}.
L001206	We use PHOTOS~\cite{Barberio:1993qi} to simulate final-state radiation and GEANT3~\cite{Brun:1987ma} to model the response of the Belle detector.
L001207	
L001208	To characterize background rates, estimate systematic uncertainties, and validate our analysis procedure,
L001209	we study cascade decays of $\cgy{J}$ with subsequent $\yee$ or $\ymm$ decays as our control modes.
L001210	These control modes are chosen because direct decays of $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ to same-flavor lepton pairs proceed via two photons or the Higgs boson in the SM,
L001211	and have branching fractions of ${\mathcal{O}}(10^{-20})\text{--}{\mathcal{O}}(10^{-9})$~\cite{Godfrey:2015vda}, making them unmeasurable.
L001212	In the searches for $\chi_{bJ}(1P) \to e^\pm \tau^\mp$ or $\mu^\pm \tau^\mp$, we reconstruct the $\tm$ and $\te$ decay modes, respectively,
L001213	to minimize backgrounds from $e^+ e^- \to \ell^+ \ell^-$ events, where $\ell = e, \mu$.
L001214	
L001215	We select events with exactly two oppositely charged tracks, one identified as an electron and the other as a muon.
L001216	Each track is required to have a momentum greater than 200\mev~\footnote{Natural units ($\hbar=c=1$) are used throughout this letter, and all variables are defined in the laboratory frame, unless otherwise mentioned.}
L001217	and a distance of closest approach to the $e^+e^-$ collision point of less than 4.5\cm along the $e^-$ beam direction and 1.5\cm in the plane transverse to it.
L001218	A track is identified as an electron if its electron likelihood ratio exceeds 0.5; otherwise, it is identified as a muon when its muon likelihood ratio exceeds 0.9.
L001219	The efficiencies of this selection are 92.4\% and 93.8\% for electrons and muons, respectively~\cite{Hanagaki:2001fz,Abashian:2002bd}.
L001220	We recover the energy that an electron may lose to bremsstrahlung by adding to its four-momentum the four-momenta of photon candidates found within $60~{\rm{mrad}}$ of its momentum.
L001221	To make the datasets mutually exclusive, we require the momenta of electrons or muons from $\cb{J}$ decays exceed 3.5\gev and that from $\tau$ decays fall below 3.5\gev.
L001222	
L001223	All photons must have energies above 50\mev\ to reduce the contamination from beam background.
L001224	The energies of the photons from $\Upsilon(2S)\to\gamma\chi_{bJ}(1P)$ and $\cgy{J}$ decays, labeled $E_{{\gamma}_1}$ and $E_{{\gamma}_2}$, respectively,
L001225	are known to mostly satisfy the relation $E_{{\gamma}_1} <  E_{{\gamma}_2}$~\cite{CLEO:2010xuh, BaBar:2014och}.
L001226	To reduce combinatorial background, we require $E_{{\gamma}_1}$ $<$ 250\mev and $E_{{\gamma}_2}$ $>$ 250\mev.
L001227	
L001228	For the $\cb{J}\to e^\pm\mu^\mp$ decays and the control modes, a four-constraint kinematic fit is performed
L001229	using energy-momentum conservation and the measured uncertainties of the final-state momenta.
L001230	Kinematically inconsistent events are suppressed by a loose requirement on the fit $\chi^2$ value, $\chi^2_{\rm 4C} < 1000$, a selection that retains nearly all signal decays.
L001231	
L001232	The control mode $\chi_{bJ}(1P)\to\gamma e^+e^-$ is mimicked by the radiative Bhabha process,
L001233	whose cross section is significantly larger than that for $\Upsilon(2S)$ production.
L001234	A veto that rejected 99\% of Bhabha events was applied during data taking~\cite{CHEON2002548}.
L001235	To further suppress such events, we remove regions dominated by Bhabha backgrounds,
L001236	characterized by $\theta_{e^-}<0.75~\mathrm{rad}$ and $\theta_{\gamma_1}<0.6~\mathrm{rad}$, or $\theta_{\gamma_1}>2.2~\mathrm{rad}$,
L001237	where $\theta_{e^-}$ and $\theta_{\gamma_1}$ are the angles of the final-state electron and the $\gamma_1$ candidate with respect to the electron beam.
L001238	Additionally, we reject any event with an $e^-$ track with momentum above $6.4\mathrm{GeV}$.
L001239	We estimate the efficiency of the control mode using events that pass a dedicated simulation of the Bhabha veto.
L001240	
L001241	In terms of the total energy $E_{X}$ and momentum ${\vec{p}}_{X}$ of a set of particles ${X}$,
L001242	the mass recoiling against that set is given by
L001243	\begin{equation}
L001244	M^{\rm{recoil}}_{X} = \sqrt{(E_{e^+e^-}-E_{X})^2-({\vec{p}}_{e^+e^-}-{\vec{p}}_{X})^2},
L001245	\label{eq:mrecoil}
L001246	\end{equation}
L001247	where $E_{e^+e^-}$ and ${\vec{p}}_{e^+e^-}$ are the total energy and momentum of the colliding electron and positron.
L001248	Control events are required to have $\mrgg$ in [9.43, 9.51]\gev, encompassing the $\y1$ resonance.
L001249	Both control and signal events are required to have $\mrg$ in [9.81, 9.95]\gev, covering the $\cb{J}$ states.
L001250	Also, we require $M^{\rm{recoil}}_{\gamma_1\ell_1}$ to be in [0.6, 2.8]\gev, which extends approximately 2.5 times the mass resolution in either direction of the tau mass, for $\chi_{bJ}(1P)\to\ell_1^\pm\tau^\mp$ searches, where $\ell_1$ refers to the higher-energy lepton in the final state.
L001251	
L001252	In 40\% of events, multiple $\y2$ candidates are formed due to photons from beam background and final-state radiation.
L001253	For $\cem J$ and the control modes, we retain the candidate with the lowest $\chifc$, which selects the true candidate 98\% of the time in simulation.
L001254	For $\chi_{bJ}(1P)\to \ell_1^\pm \tau^\mp$, we retain the candidate whose $\gamma_1$ energy yields an $M^{\rm recoil}_{\gamma_1\ell_1}$ value closest to the known $\tau$ mass~\cite{Belle-II:2023izd}, selecting the true candidate 75\% of the time. In simulated events, best-candidate selection does not bias the analysis results.
L001255	
