L001350	The branching fraction $\mt{B}(\chi_{bJ}(1P)\to\ell_1\ell_2)$ is obtained from the number of signal events $N_{J, \ell_1\ell_2}$ using
L001351	\begin{equation}
L001352	\mt{B}(\chi_{bJ}(1P)\to\ell_1\ell_2) = \frac{N_{J, \ell_1\ell_2}/N_{\y2}}{\mt{B}(\gc{J})\epsilon_{J, \ell_1\ell_2}}.
L001353	\label{equ:ul}
L001354	\end{equation}
L001355	Here $\epsilon_{J, \ell_1\ell_2}$ is the efficiency to reconstruct $\gamma\LL$ via the $\chi_{bJ}(1P)$, including the relevant $\tau$ branching fractions.
L001356	
L001357	As all signal yields are consistent with zero, we calculate upper limits on branching fractions using the CLs method~\cite{CLs:2011}.
L001358	For $\cem{J}$, we use \pyhf~\cite{Heinrich:2021gyp} and for $\clt{J}$, we use a frequentist calculator from {\tt RooStats}~\cite{Moneta:2010pm}.
L001359	In each case, an ensemble of 5000 pseudo-experiments is used.
L001360	Systematic uncertainties are included by smearing the yields.
